            <div xml:id="d1742">

                <pb facs="1115" n="999" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg999"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 718.1915/223.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister of Costa Rica to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of Costa Rica</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 7, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Secretary of State</hi>: In compliance with
                    special instructions from my Government, I have the honor to forward to your
                    excellency a copy of the award of the Chief Justice of the United States, which
                        <pb facs="1116" n="1000" xml:id="pg1000"/> brought to a happy end the
                    boundary dispute between Costa Rica and Panama.</p>
                <p>It gives the Government of my country extreme pleasure to express, most
                    cordially, its gratitude to the Government of your excellency, through whose
                    efficacious mediation the Anderson-Porras Treaty was concluded. And it also
                    gladly recalls on this occasion that through the ever friendly intercession of
                    the United States the award rendered by the illustrious President Cleveland, and
                    also the services of General Alexander in the physical marking of the boundary,
                    settled once for all time, the boundary dispute with the Republic of Nicaragua,
                    which contributed in no small measure to secure the maintenance of strong and
                    unalterable harmony. And it is now my Government’s most earnest hope that, the
                    award of the Chief Justice of the United States having forever settled the
                    almost century-old boundary question, the international difficulties with the
                    Republic of Colombia and later with that of Panama, formerly frequent, have
                    finally vanished.</p>
                <p>I avail [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">R. Brenes Mesén</hi></signed>.</closer>


                <xi:include href="d1742-a1.xml" xpointer="d1742-a1"/>
